Title: "Syntax of Propositional Formulas"
Id: "013e019e-95a0-4f56-9ab7-22549bbdd013"


Text: "Every formal mathematical language begins with an explicit, unambiguous
       specification of its vocabulary and grammatical formation rules. The
       syntax of propositional logic is designed so that whether a symbolic
       string is grammatically admissible is entirely decidable through
       combinatorial rules, completely independent of any consideration of truth
       or meaning."
Id: "62b46f95-2a7d-4660-91cb-5d63953f76f9"


[\prop.variable]
Declares: p is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "propositional variable"
. description: "An atomic propositional symbol that serves as an indivisible
                building block for compound assertions."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{14}
Id: "2aaa0620-6cbe-4451-9aec-3ab12757a1ef"


Text: "From atomic propositional variables, we construct compound formulas
       through the application of logical connectives according to explicit
       inductive rules. The resulting well-formed expressions constitute the
       formal language of propositional logic."
Id: "7eb84402-2692-4456-ab73-35783d2364c8"


[\prop.formula]
Declares: alpha is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "well-formed propositional formula"
. written: "\alpha"
. description: "A grammatically well-formed propositional formula generated
                inductively from propositional variables and Boolean
                connectives."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{14}
Id: "3a01676a-6b51-4bd2-8940-9e7a4949505b"


Text: "To analyze the depth and structure of a formula, we assign each
       well-formed formula an integer complexity, measuring the total count of
       logical connectives it contains."
Id: "8a065d82-8539-48b4-8099-50036be49510"


[\prop.complexity{A}]
Defines: c is \integer
when: A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "complexity of formula $A?$"
. written: "\operatorname{comp}(A?)"
. description: "The total number of logical connective occurrences in formula A,
                used as the metric for induction on formula length."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{14}
Id: "a1cd63dd-e94b-49b5-ac88-52d38b879e6a"


Text: "Every formula can be uniquely decomposed into its constituent
       subformulas. The Unique Readability Theorem guarantees that propositional
       formulas are unambiguous: there is exactly one way to parse any
       well-formed formula."
Id: "6a04adc9-26e3-4469-ae00-1e1d9c22aaa5"


Theorem:
given: alpha is \\statement
then:
. anyOf:
  . exists: p is \prop.variable
    suchThat: alpha = p
  . exists: beta is \\statement
    suchThat: alpha = \not{beta}
  . exists: beta is \\statement
    suchThat:
    . exists: gamma is \\statement
      suchThat:
      . anyOf:
        . alpha = (beta \.and./ gamma)
        . alpha = (beta \.or./ gamma)
        . alpha = (beta \.implies./ gamma)
        . alpha = (beta \.iff./ gamma)
Documented:
. called: "unique readability of propositional formulas"
. description: "Every well-formed propositional formula is either an atomic
                variable, a negation, or a unique binary connective combination
                of subformulas."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{15}
Id: "bf33a971-1761-4f9b-b30a-76272f77f316"


Text: "The inductive definition of formulas gives rise to a universal proof
       technique: structural induction. To verify that a mathematical property
       holds for all formulas, it suffices to prove the property for atomic
       variables and establish that each connective preserves the property."
Id: "ccfb1b84-98e4-419d-b075-3c3231c761f1"


Theorem:
given: P(x_) is (_ is \\statement) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. forAll: p is \prop.variable
  then: P(p)
. forAll: alpha is \\statement
  where: P(alpha)
  then: P(\not{alpha})
. forAll: alpha is \\statement
  then:
  . forAll: beta is \\statement
    where:
    . P(alpha)
    . P(beta)
    then: P(alpha \.and./ beta)
then:
. forAll: alpha is \\statement
  then: P(alpha)
Documented:
. called: "principle of structural induction for propositional formulas"
. description: "If a property holds for all atomic propositional variables and
                is preserved under negation and binary connectives, then it
                holds for every well-formed formula."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{16}
Id: "f7ef750c-03f4-4777-9027-fce1af244783"
